the risky journey across the Mediterranean to southern Europe for years, but authorities have reported a sharp increase this month in the numbers in need of rescue. The number of deaths has also skyrocketed. The International Organization for Migration said Monday that more than 1,100 people were estimated to have drowned in the waters between Libya and Italy just in the past week. It reported Monday that three other migrant boats could be in distress in international waters. Shipping companies recently warned that European governments are putting what they say is an unfair burden on their vessels to come to the aid of migrant boats in trouble. \\\"We believe it is unacceptable that the international community is increasingly relying on merchant ships and seafarers to undertake more and more large-scale rescues, with single ships having to rescue as many 500 people at a time,\\\" the European Community Shipowners Associations said in a letter to EU leaders last month. \\\"Commercial ships are not equipped to undertake such large-scale rescues, which also create serious risks to the safety, health and welfare of ships' crews who should not be expected to deal which such situations,\\\" the letter said. Commercial ships rescued more than 40,000 people in the Mediterranean last year, according to the International Organization for Migration. That's higher than the more than 35,000 people saved by the Italian Coast Guard but lower than the 80,000 rescued by the Italian Navy, which was running a special program, Mare Nostrum, for most of the year. European Union ministers met Monday in Luxembourg and proposed a 10-point plan to help address the crisis. \\\"We are not yet working on numbers, but what we have agreed on today is, for sure, the need to increase significantly the resources at sea, and the level of the operation, doing more search and rescue and doing it more together,\\\" said Federica Mogherini, the EU high representative for foreign affairs and security police. Mogherini told CNN that the European Union must fight human traffickers, strengthen Europeans' duty to save lives at sea and share responsibility when it comes to the resettlement and relocation of refugees. \\\"We need to fight the organizations that are trafficking and smuggling people, so that we can prevent desperate people from leaving in desperate conditions,\\\" Mogherini said. \\\"My pain is that it was a reaction coming too late after so many people died.\\\" But some groups said European officials were offering too little too late. \\\"What we needed from EU foreign ministers today was life-saving action, but they dithered,\\\" said Justin Forsyth, the chief executive of Save The Children. \\\"With each day we delay we lose more innocent lives and Europe slips further into an immoral abyss,\\\" Forsyth said in a statement. \\\"Right now, people desperately seeking a better life are drowning in politics.\\\" CNN's Karl Penhaul reported from Catania, and Jethro Mullen reported and wrote from Hong Kong. CNN's Hada Messia, Isa Soares and Atika Shubert contributed to this report.\\n\",\"answer\":\"The recent shipwreck in the Mediterranean that resulted in the deaths of hundreds of migrants has sparked controversy over the role of commercial vessels in rescuing migrants at sea. According to the United Nations' refugee agency, the boat may have capsized after being touched or swamped by a cargo ship that came to its aid. Survivors have told the UNHCR that they were crowded on the highest part of the boat, while many others were trapped in the lower areas. Italian authorities, however, have disputed this version of events, claiming that the migrants themselves caused the boat to capsize by rushing to one side. The cargo ship, King Jacob, has been accused of causing the tragedy, but the company that manages it has denied these allegations. The incident has highlighted the challenges faced by commercial vessels in responding to the escalating migrant crisis in the Mediterranean. Shipping companies have complained that they are being unfairly burdened with the responsibility of rescuing migrants, and have called for greater support from European governments. The EU has proposed a 10-point plan to address the crisis, including increasing resources at sea and sharing responsibility for the resettlement and relocation of refugees. However, some groups have criticized the plan as too little, too late.\",\"source_segments\":[{\"index\":0,\"byte_start\":0,\"byte_end\":206},{\"index\":1,\"byte_start\":206,\"byte_end\":221},{\"index\":2,\"byte_start\":221,\"byte_end\":448},{\"index\":3,\"byte_start\":448,\"byte_end\":673},{\"index\":4,\"byte_start\":673,\"byte_end\":758},{\"index\":5,\"byte_start\":758,\"byte_end\":930},{\"index\":6,\"byte_start\":930,\"byte_end\":1110},{\"index\":7,\"byte_start\":1110,\"byte_end\":1213},{\"index\":8,\"byte_start\":1213,\"byte_end\":1309},{\"index\":9,\"byte_start\":1309,\"byte_end\":1376},{\"index\":10,\"byte_start\":1376,\"byte_end\":1571},{\"index\":11,\"byte_start\":1571,\"byte_end\":1677},{\"index\":12,\"byte_start\":1677,\"byte_end\":1848},{\"index\":13,\"byte_start\":1848,\"byte_end\":2070},{\"index\":14,\"byte_start\":2070,\"byte_end\":2182},{\"index\":15,\"byte_start\":2182,\"byte_end\":2306},{\"index\":16,\"byte_start\":2306,\"byte_end\":2445},{\"index\":17,\"byte_start\":2445,\"byte_end\":2573},{\"index\":18,\"byte_start\":2573,\"byte_end\":2648},{\"index\":19,\"byte_start\":2648,\"byte_end\":2813},{\"index\":20,\"byte_start\":2813,\"byte_end\":2982},{\"index\":21,\"byte_start\":2982,\"byte_end\":3143},{\"index\":22,\"byte_start\":3143,\"byte_end\":3243},{\"index\":23,\"byte_start\":3243,\"byte_end\":3416},{\"index\":24,\"byte_start\":3416,\"byte_end\":3584},{\"index\":25,\"byte_start\":3584,\"byte_end\":3826},{\"index\":26,\"byte_start\":3826,\"byte_end\":3869},{\"index\":27,\"byte_start\":3869,\"byte_end\":4046},{\"index\":28,\"byte_start\":4046,\"byte_end\":4142},{\"index\":29,\"byte